Use `NodejsFunction` from `aws-cdk-lib/aws-lambda-nodejs`, which bundles during CDK synthesis, and pin CDK as `aws/aws-cdk@v2` [2][V].

Based on [3]:
```ts
new nodejs.NodejsFunction(this, 'my-handler', {
  entry: 'handler/index.ts',
  bundling: {
    preCompilation: true,
  },
});
```

- `preCompilation: true` enables a `tsc` pre-compilation step [3].
- A `tsconfig.json` file is required [3].

If you want custom bundling during synthesis, the library also shows using your own build script with `NodejsFunction` [2].

Use the CDK `BucketDeployment` construct to populate an S3 bucket with files from a local directory, and pin CDK to `aws/aws-cdk@v2` [1][V].

Based on [1]:
```ts
const websiteBucket = new s3.Bucket(this, 'WebsiteBucket', {
  websiteIndexDocument: 'index.html',
  publicReadAccess: true,
});

new s3deploy.BucketDeployment(this, 'DeployWebsite', {
  sources: [s3deploy.Source.asset('./website-dist')],
  destinationBucket: websiteBucket,
  destinationKeyPrefix: 'web/static',
});
```

`BucketDeployment` uploads the local directory contents to an intermediary CDK assets bucket and then syncs them into the destination bucket [1].

If you are setting up the bucket for static website hosting, the docs say to enable static website hosting and enter the exact index document name, such as `index.html` [2].

