Use a CloudFront distribution with an S3 origin and origin access control (OAC), because OAC sends authenticated requests through CloudFront so viewers can’t access the S3 bucket directly [3].

- The CloudFront tutorial says its standard distribution uses OAC to send authenticated requests to the S3 origin and prevent viewers from accessing the bucket directly [3].
- If you want the bucket to stay private, CloudFront’s private content feature can restrict access to content in the S3 bucket [1].
- Grant CloudFront access to the bucket with a bucket policy that allows the CloudFront service principal `cloudfront.amazonaws.com` and scopes access with the distribution ARN in `AWS:SourceArn` [5].
- If you use the legacy OAI method instead, you must give the OAI permission in the bucket policy and specify the correct OAI as the `Principal` [4].

Based on [5]:
```json
{
  "Version":"2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "AllowCloudFrontServicePrincipalReadWrite",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "Service": "cloudfront.amazonaws.com"
      },
      "Action": [
        "s3:GetObject",
        "s3:PutObject"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::amzn-s3-demo-bucket/*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "AWS:SourceArn": "arn:aws:cloudfront::111122223333:distribution/{{CloudFront-distribution-ID}}"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

If you do not define a default root object, requests for the root of the distribution pass to the origin server, and with an S3 origin CloudFront may return a list of bucket contents or private contents depending on configuration [2].
